---
import SubsMock from './mocks/SubsMock.astro'
---

  <section class="feat" id="subs">
    <div class="wrap feat-in">
      <div class="feat-copy" data-reveal>
        <h2>Subtitles that are already in sync</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Frame Player searches OpenSubtitles by the file's hash, which matches your exact release instead of guessing from the title. Title search is the fallback, and the panel says which one you got.
        </p>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>A frame-rate mismatch is flagged before you download</b> — a 25 fps subtitle on a 23.976 fps file drifts further out the longer you watch.</span></li>
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>Subtitles move above the controls</b> while the bar is up, and a delay you set is kept for that file.</span></li>
          <li><svg class="ic" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-check"/></svg>
            <span><b>The dub you picked in episode 1 is picked again in episode 2</b>, matched by language and title, not track number.</span></li>
        </ul>
        <a class="more-link" href="/guides/subtitles-out-of-sync">Why subtitles drift out of sync</a>
      </div>
      <div class="feat-mock" data-reveal style="--dl:1">
        <SubsMock />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
